You are assisting with the OpenCourts.fyi project. Your task is to read four local files:

1. software-design-document.md  
2. software-requirements-specification.md  
3. project-milestones.md  
4. volunteer-activity-categories.md  

You will then generate a complete set of GitHub issues that, when implemented, will produce an MVP that satisfies every requirement in the software-requirements-specification.md.

Your output must follow these rules:

----------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 1 — INPUT INTERPRETATION RULES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Read all four files carefully before generating any issues.
2. Treat the SRS as the source of truth for required functionality.
3. Treat the SDD as the source of truth for system design and task structure.
4. Treat project-milestones.md as the authoritative list of Milestones.
5. Treat volunteer-activity-categories.md as the authoritative list of Activity Types.
6. Do NOT invent features, requirements, or repositories.
7. Do NOT merge or reinterpret requirements. Translate them exactly.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 2 — ISSUE GENERATION RULES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

For each SDD entry, generate a set of small, atomic, volunteer-friendly issues.

Each issue must:

1. Be implementable in 2–6 hours by volunteers with varying technical experience.
2. Be routed to one of the official OpenCourts.fyi repositories:
   - opencourts-infra
   - opencourts-etl
   - opencourts-ckan
   - opencourts-mock-website
   - opencourts-governance

3. Include the following fields:

- **Title:**  
  A short, actionable description of the task.

- **Body:**  
  Must include:
  - The SDD Feature Name  
  - The Problem Statement  
  - The specific task to be completed  
  - Acceptance Criteria (required)  
  - Any Dependencies listed in the SDD  

- **Repository:**  
  One of the five OpenCourts.fyi repos, chosen strictly according to the SDD’s Target Repository field.

- **Milestone:**  
  One value from project-milestones.md.  
  Do NOT invent or modify milestone names.

- **Activity Type:**  
  One value from volunteer-activity-categories.md.  
  Do NOT invent or modify activity types.

- **Labels:**  
  Use only the labels listed in the SDD entry.

- **Priority:**  
  Copy the priority from the SDD entry.

4. Issues must be written in a structured, machine-readable JSON array.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 3 — ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA RULES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Every issue MUST include Acceptance Criteria that are:

- Testable  
- Unambiguous  
- Derived strictly from the SRS and SDD  
- Free of assumptions or invented behaviors  

Acceptance Criteria must be written as bullet points beginning with “- [ ]”.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 4 — OUTPUT FORMAT
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Output a single JSON array.  
Each element must be a GitHub issue object with the following fields:

{
  "title": "...",
  "body": "...",
  "repo": "...",
  "milestone": "...",
  "activity_type": "...",
  "labels": [...],
  "priority": "High | Medium | Low"
}

Do NOT include commentary outside the JSON.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 5 — EXECUTION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Read the four local files.  
2. Extract all requirements, design elements, milestones, and activity types.  
3. Generate a complete set of issues that, when implemented, will produce an MVP that satisfies the SRS.  
4. Ensure full coverage: every SRS requirement must map to at least one issue.  
5. Ensure no hallucination: do not add features, repos, or categories not present in the files.